{"content":"# 💡 SUGGESTION: fail closed when the head probe fails — right now the sweep continues with both protective gates dead

```javascript
if (!head) {
    probedHead = prHeadAndLabels(repoInfo, prNumber);
    if (probedHead) head = probedHead.headSha;
}
if (consultReworkLatch(key, ticket, repoInfo, head).latched) return false;
if (head && hasActiveLegRun(...)) { ... return false; }
```

When `prHeadAndLabels` fails (transient `github_get_pr` error) and the item carries no head, `head` stays `null` — and the sweep then proceeds with the gh-806 latch consult short-circuited to not-latched and the in-flight leg-run gate skipped, all the way to a possible restore (and an `armReworkLatch(ticket, null)` no-op, so the restored arm gets no latch protection either). The consequence is bounded — a possible stacked leg, retried correctly next tick — but it is the only place in this otherwise consistently fail-closed machinery where a read failure degrades toward action instead of toward quiet, and it contradicts the pure module's own doctrine ("no clock, no restore").

One line restores the invariant:

```javascript
if (!head) {
    console.log('  ⏭️  ' + key + ' sweep: head unavailable (item.pr null, fresh probe failed) — fail closed, retry next tick');
    return false;
}
```
